course: dictionary
lesson: 1488
title: Causeways and clues
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words name things you may never have seen. When you meet one, read the words around it, because they are its context and often leave clues. «Their capital, Tenochtitlan, had causeways, canals, floating gardens and a huge market.» Canals are waterways, and floating gardens sit on water, so a causeway must be something built across the water or the wet ground.
explain: Try your guess in the sentence. Put in "a raised road across water" in place of causeway and see if it still makes sense. It does, so your guess is probably right. Then check with the dictionary: a causeway is a raised road or path across wet ground, marshes or shallow water.
explain: The word also appears in a second sentence: «Tenochtitlan, the Aztec capital, was built on a lake with causeways, canals, floating gardens and a huge market.» The lake is the second clue. A raised road over a lake would keep people dry and let them travel between islands in the city.
fact: A causeway is a raised road or path across wet ground, marshes or shallow water.
fact: Holy Island in England is joined to the mainland by a causeway that floods at high tide.
fact: Always check the tide times before crossing a tidal causeway.
fact: The Giant's Causeway in Northern Ireland has about 40,000 six-sided rock columns.
fact: The Aztec capital Tenochtitlan had causeways, canals and floating gardens, and Mexico City stands on its ruins.
exercise: «Tenochtitlan had causeways, canals, floating gardens and a huge market.» What is a causeway? | A deep hole dug to hold water | *A raised road or path across wet ground or shallow water | A kind of boat with a sail | A tall tower built to watch the sea
exercise: Holy Island in England is joined to the mainland by a causeway. When is it dangerous to cross? | When the tide is out | *When the tide is high and the causeway floods | When the sky is blue | When it is a school holiday
exercise: «Tenochtitlan had causeways, canals, floating gardens and a huge market.» Find and copy one word that names a waterway. | capital | gardens | *canals | market
test: «The Giant's Causeway in Northern Ireland has about 40,000 six-sided rock columns.» How many rock columns does the sentence mention? | *40,000 | 4,000,000 | 4,000 | 400
test: «The city had causeways, canals and floating gardens.» Which is the best meaning of causeway? | A garden that floats on a lake | *A raised road or path across wet ground or shallow water | A type of fish that lives in lakes | A tall stone tower
exam: Which is the best way to check a guess at a new word? | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Read the word aloud ten times | Ask someone to write the word for you | Skip the sentence and never read it again
